# The surface other machines talk to.
#
# It serves the same routes as the public site, but its certificate always
# comes from the local authority — never from UT_CERT_DIR, never from a public
# ACME provider. The two are different questions: what a browser must trust,
# and what one machine must prove to another.
#
# Keeping it here is also what keeps the authority alive. In UT_INGRESS_MODE
# "custom" or "edge" the public certificate comes from the customer, so the
# authority would issue nothing and rot until the day a second machine needs
# enrolling.
#
# It carries its own hostname rather than the apex: Caddy refuses two
# certificate policies for one name, and the public site already holds the
# apex. compose gives the proxy that name as a network alias on the authority's
# network, so the ACME challenge resolves.
#
# Bound to loopback by default. A customer whose load balancer wants to verify
# the appliance rather than trust it blindly sets UT_INTERNAL_BIND_ADDRESS to
# the interface it reaches, and adds the authority root to that balancer.
#
# Mount caddy/no-internal-surface.caddy instead to switch it off — a compute
# node runs no authority of its own and would retry ACME forever.

https://{$UT_NODE_NAME:node}.{$UT_DOMAIN:understand.local}:{$UT_INTERNAL_PORT:8443} {
    tls {
        issuer acme {
            dir https://step-ca:9000/acme/acme/directory
            trusted_roots /etc/caddy/ca/certs/root_ca.crt
        }
    }

    import platform_routes
}
